data: {"type":"response.output_text.done","sequence_number":2627,"item_id":"msg_0616f87d83e66315016adb2f476d70819197d6163e78adbb2f","output_index":1,"content_index":0,"text":"{\"status\":\"COMPLETED_PROOF_CANDIDATE\",\"target\":\"For every nonnegative integer n and every nonempty compact E⊆ℝⁿ, with I=[0,1] and E×I equipped with the metric induced by concatenated Euclidean coordinates, dim_H(E×I)=dim_H(E)+1.\",\"obstacle_assessment\":{\"classification\":\"ROUTINE_COMPLETION\",\"reasoning\":\"The reported issue is a correctable direction-of-inclusion error, not a substantive gap. For each t∈I, the indices with t∈B_i cover E, because every (x,t) belongs to some V_i. Since B_i⊆J_i, those indices are among the indices with t∈J_i. Thus the latter indices also cover E. The pointwise content inequality follows, and integrating its measurable right-hand side is valid even if the projections B_i are nonmeasurable. The arbitrary-cover argument then gives the lower bound. The proof below supplies that repair explicitly and handles zero diameters and the D=0 case.\"},\"complete_proof\":\"For A in a metric space, write c_a(A,δ) for the infimum of Σ_j diam(W_j)^a over countable covers of A by sets W_j of diameter at most δ. We use the defining Hausdorff-measure facts that Hausdorff measure is the limit, as δ decreases to zero, of these contents (up to a fixed positive normalization), and that dim_H(A) is its critical exponent. In particular, if a>dim_H(A), then H^a(A)=0; if 0≤a<dim_H(A), then H^a(A)=∞. These threshold facts are used below with the exponents indicated.\\n\\nPut D=dim_H(E) and F=E×[0,1]. Distances in F are the concatenated Euclidean distances: d((x,t),(y,u))=(|x−y|²+|t−u|²)^(1/2). Since E is compact it is bounded. A bounded subset of ℝⁿ has finite Hausdorff dimension: for each integer m>n, cover a bounded box containing it by O(ℓ^(−n)) cubes of side ℓ. Each cube has diameter at most √nℓ, so the total m-power cost is O(ℓ^(m−n)), which tends to zero as ℓ tends to zero. (For n=0 the space is a singleton and the same finiteness is immediate.) Thus D is finite.\\n\\nUpper bound. Fix a real q>D. Then q>0 and H^q(E)=0. For every δ>0 and every positive error bound, the definition of Hausdorff measure supplies a countable cover of E by sets of diameter at most δ with arbitrarily small total q-power cost. Empty sets can be discarded. If a covering set is a singleton, replace it by a ball centered at that point of positive diameter at most δ. For countably many such replacements, choose the diameters so their q-power sum is arbitrarily small (for example, bound the jth added q-power by η2^(−j), for any η>0). Consequently E has covers with positive diameters r_j≤δ and arbitrarily small Σ_j r_j^q.\\n\\nTake δ≤1. For each j, partition [0,1] into at most 1/r_j+1 intervals of length at most r_j. The products of the jth cover set with these intervals cover that set times [0,1]. Each product has diameter at most √2 r_j: for any two of its points, the squared product distance is at most r_j²+r_j². The sum of the (q+1)-powers of the diameters of these product sets is at most\\n\\nΣ_j (1/r_j+1)(√2 r_j)^(q+1) ≤ 2(√2)^(q+1) Σ_j r_j^q,\\n\\nusing r_j≤1. Their diameters are at most √2δ. Since the original total q-cost can be made arbitrarily small, c_(q+1)(F,√2δ)=0 for each such δ, and hence H^(q+1)(F)=0. Therefore dim_H(F)≤q+1. This holds for every q>D; letting q decrease to D gives dim_H(F)≤D+1.\\n\\nLower bound when D>0. Fix any real s with 0<s<D. Then H^s(E)=∞, and thus c_s(E,δ) tends to infinity as δ decreases to zero. Fix 0<δ≤1 and any countable cover (V_i) of F by sets of diameter ρ_i≤δ. Discard empty members. Let A_i and B_i be the projections of V_i onto ℝⁿ and ℝ, respectively. Coordinate projection does not increase Euclidean distance, so diam(A_i)≤ρ_i and diam(B_i)≤ρ_i. For each i choose an open interval J_i containing B_i whose length is at most ρ_i+η_i, with η_i>0. Such an interval exists because B_i is nonempty and bounded and has diameter at most ρ_i. Choose the η_i so that Σ_i η_iρ_i^s<ε, for any prescribed ε>0; for instance, after enumerating the cover, take η_i=ε2^(−i)/(1+ρ_i^s).\\n\\nFor each t∈[0,1], the sets A_i for which t∈B_i cover E: given x∈E, the point (x,t) belongs to some V_i, so x∈A_i and t∈B_i. As B_i⊆J_i, the sets A_i for which t∈J_i also cover E. They have diameters at most ρ_i≤δ. By the definition of c_s(E,δ), for every t∈[0,1],\\n\\nc_s(E,δ) ≤ Σ_i 1_{J_i}(t)ρ_i^s.\\n\\nThe right-hand side is a measurable nonnegative series, since each J_i is open. Integrate over [0,1]. The interval has length one, so the integral of the constant left-hand side is c_s(E,δ). By Tonelli’s theorem for nonnegative measurable functions, the integral of the series is the sum of its integrals. Moreover, |J_i∩[0,1]|≤ρ_i+η_i. It follows that\\n\\nc_s(E,δ) ≤ Σ_i |J_i∩[0,1]|ρ_i^s ≤ Σ_i ρ_i^(s+1)+Σ_i η_iρ_i^s ≤ Σ_i ρ_i^(s+1)+ε.\\n\\nThis argument uses no measurability of B_i. If ρ_i=0, its displayed contributions vanish because s>0. Since ε was arbitrary, c_s(E,δ)≤Σ_iρ_i^(s+1) for every such cover. Taking the infimum over the covers of F gives c_s(E,δ)≤c_(s+1)(F,δ). As δ decreases to zero, c_s(E,δ) tends to infinity, so c_(s+1)(F,δ) does also. Hence H^(s+1)(F)=∞ and dim_H(F)≥s+1. This holds for every 0<s<D; taking the supremum over those s yields dim_H(F)≥D+1.\\n\\nLower bound when D=0. Since E is nonempty, choose x₀∈E. The coordinate projection p:F→[0,1], p(x,t)=t, is 1-Lipschitz: |t−u|≤(|x−y|²+|t−u|²)^(1/2). It is onto because p(x₀,t)=t for every t∈[0,1]. The Lipschitz dimension inequality gives dim_H([0,1])≤dim_H(F). The standard interval dimension fact dim_H([0,1])=1 therefore gives dim_H(F)≥1=D+1.\\n\\nCombining the lower bound in both cases with the upper bound proves dim_H(E×[0,1])=dim_H(E)+1. The argument includes n=0: ℝ⁰ is a singleton, the bounded-set finiteness assertion still holds, and nonemptiness makes the projection onto [0,1] available.\",\"source_obligations\":[{\"fact\":\"Hausdorff dimension threshold: H^q(E)=0 for q>dim_H(E), and H^s(E)=∞ for 0≤s<dim_H(E).\",\"status\":\"Imported general Hausdorff-dimension fact; actual mathlib declarations were inspected.\",\"details\":\"The inspected Mathlib/Topology/MetricSpace/HausdorffDimension.lean defines dimH and includes hausdorffMeasure_of_lt_dimH and hausdorffMeasure_of_dimH_lt. The first gives infinite measure below dimension; the second gives zero measure above dimension. A complete formal application still requires matching the task’s notation/metric and checking all instance and exponent conversions.\"},{\"fact\":\"Hausdorff measure is the small-scale limit of covering contents, and zero/infinite measure has the stated consequences for contents.\",\"status\":\"Definition-level Hausdorff-measure facts.\",\"details\":\"The proof uses that zero measure permits arbitrarily small costs at any fixed positive scale, and infinite measure forces the scale contents to be unbounded as the scale tends to zero. These follow from the definition as the limit of the scale contents; the normalization factor is fixed and positive.\"},{\"fact\":\"Tonelli’s theorem for a nonnegative measurable countable series.\",\"status\":\"Imported general measure-theoretic fact.\",\"details\":\"Applied only to the functions t↦1_{J_i}(t)ρ_i^s on [0,1]. Each is measurable and nonnegative. The supplied task material identifies a mathlib candidate, but this artifact does not claim a verified complete signature or formal application.\"},{\"fact\":\"Lipschitz maps do not increase Hausdorff dimension, and dim_H([0,1])=1.\",\"status\":\"Imported general Hausdorff-dimension facts; formal source applicability remains an obligation.\",\"details\":\"The supplied materials identify mathlib candidates LipschitzWith.dimH_image_le and Real.dimH_of_nonempty_interior. Their complete signatures and application conditions must be checked in a formal handoff; no formal acceptance is claimed.\"},{\"fact\":\"A bounded subset of ℝⁿ has finite Hausdorff dimension.\",\"status\":\"Elementary derivation given in the proof.\",\"details\":\"A bounded box can be covered by O(ℓ^(−n)) cubes of side ℓ. For any integer m>n, their total m-power diameter cost is O(ℓ^(m−n)), tending to zero. The n=0 case is a singleton.\"},{\"fact\":\"Product diameter bound and coordinate projections are 1-Lipschitz.\",\"status\":\"Elementary derivation given in the proof.\",\"details\":\"The squared concatenated Euclidean distance is the sum of squared coordinate distances. Taking coordinate distances separately gives the Lipschitz bounds, and bounding each coordinate difference by its factor-set diameter gives the product diameter estimate.\"},{\"fact\":\"Covering [0,1] by at most 1/r+1 intervals of length at most r for r>0.\",\"status\":\"Elementary subdivision calculation.\",\"details\":\"Partition the interval into ⌈1/r⌉ intervals of equal length when r≤1; the number is at most 1/r+1 and each length is at most r.\"}],\"edge_cases\":[\"Nonemptiness is essential with dim_H(∅)=0; the theorem is stated only for nonempty E.\",\"When D=0, the positive-exponent slicing argument is not used; the onto coordinate projection supplies the lower bound.\",\"When n=0, the ambient space is a singleton; bounded-set finiteness and the nonempty projection argument remain valid.\",\"Zero-diameter sets in the upper-bound cover are replaced by positive-diameter balls with arbitrarily small total added q-cost. In the lower bound, zero-diameter terms have zero s-cost because s>0.\",\"Compactness is used to obtain boundedness; the cover arguments themselves need only boundedness for the finite-dimension assertion.\",\"The metric throughout is the concatenated Euclidean metric. No unproved identification with a max-product metric is used.\",\"This is a completed natural-language proof candidate, not an NL0 graph, fresh Audit A, or Lean-checked proof.\"]}","logprobs":[]}

